---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc958.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "1. Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "2. Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "3. Net Asset Classification — With vs Without Donor Restrictions" },
        { id: "s4", label: "4. Contribution Recognition — Unconditional vs Conditional Promises to Give" },
        { id: "s5", label: "5. Contributions Are Scoped Out of ASC 606 — the Nonreciprocal Transfer Distinction" },
        { id: "s6", label: "6. Donated Services and Gifts-in-Kind" },
        { id: "s7", label: "7. Functional Expense Reporting — the By-Nature-and-Function Matrix" },
        { id: "s8", label: "8. Endowments — Donor-Restricted vs Board-Designated Quasi-Endowments" },
        { id: "s9", label: "9. Worked Example — Conditional vs Unconditional Promise to Give" },
        { id: "s10", label: "10. Worked Example — Functional Expense Allocation" },
        { id: "s11", label: "11. Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s12", label: "12. Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s13", label: "13. No Dedicated IFRS Not-for-Profit Standard — Discussion" },
        { id: "s14", label: "14. Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s15", label: "15. Related Topics" },
        { id: "s16", label: "16. Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci"><div class="ctitle ci">Nonreciprocal contributions demand a genuinely different recognition trigger</div><div class="principle">Not-for-profit entities receive substantial resources through CONTRIBUTIONS — gifts, grants, pledges — where the DONOR receives NOTHING of commensurate value in return, a fundamentally NONRECIPROCAL transaction unlike an ordinary sale. ASC 958 addresses when such contributions should be RECOGNISED (Section 4), how NET ASSETS should be classified based on DONOR-imposed restrictions (Section 3), and requires a genuinely distinctive FUNCTIONAL expense presentation (Section 7).</div></div>
    <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">A genuinely underdeveloped area on the IFRS side</div><div class="principle">IFRS has NO dedicated, comprehensive standard addressing not-for-profit contribution accounting comparable to ASC 958 — a genuine, substantial GAP, not merely a difference in specific mechanics (Section 13).</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Charities, foundations, and other not-for-profit organizations</div><div class="principle">ASC 958 applies to not-for-profit entities — charities, foundations, membership organisations, religious organisations, and similar entities lacking ownership interests and operating for purposes other than to provide a return to investors.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Net Asset Classification — With vs Without Donor Restrictions</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Simplified to two categories under ASU 2016-14</div><div class="principle">ASU 2016-14 SIMPLIFIED net asset classification from the historical THREE-tier system (unrestricted / temporarily restricted / permanently restricted) down to <strong>TWO</strong> categories: NET ASSETS <strong>WITHOUT</strong> donor restrictions, and NET ASSETS <strong>WITH</strong> donor restrictions — with the TIMING and PURPOSE nuances formerly captured by the temporarily/permanently restricted distinction now conveyed primarily through ENHANCED DISCLOSURE (e.g. the nature and amount of restrictions, and when they are expected to be met) rather than through a THIRD balance sheet category.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Contribution Recognition — Unconditional vs Conditional Promises to Give</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">A genuinely distinctive recognition trigger, driven by the presence of a "barrier"</div><div class="principle">An UNCONDITIONAL promise to give is recognised as CONTRIBUTION REVENUE (and a receivable) when the PROMISE is made — even before cash is received. A CONDITIONAL promise to give — one containing a genuine "BARRIER" that must be overcome (e.g. a matching requirement, a specific measurable performance target, or a right of RETURN/RELEASE for the donor if the condition isn't met) — is recognised ONLY when the CONDITION is substantially MET, treated more like a REFUNDABLE deposit until then. Distinguishing a genuine BARRIER-based condition from a mere donor-imposed RESTRICTION (which affects net asset CLASSIFICATION, not recognition TIMING) is one of the most consequential judgement calls in this entire Topic.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Contributions Are Scoped Out of ASC 606 — the Nonreciprocal Transfer Distinction</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A separate recognition framework, deliberately outside the general revenue standard</div><div class="principle">Genuine CONTRIBUTIONS (nonreciprocal transfers, where the donor receives NOTHING of commensurate value) are explicitly SCOPED OUT of ASC 606 — recognised instead under ASC 958-605's OWN contribution recognition framework (Section 4). This distinguishes contributions from EXCHANGE transactions (e.g. a government grant that is genuinely a payment for specific services rendered, which WOULD fall under ASC 606) — requiring not-for-profits to carefully ANALYSE each resource inflow to determine which framework applies, since a grant or sponsorship arrangement can sit ambiguously between the two.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Donated Services and Gifts-in-Kind</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Recognised only under specific, narrow criteria</div><div class="principle">DONATED SERVICES are recognised ONLY if they either (a) CREATE or ENHANCE a nonfinancial asset, or (b) require SPECIALISED skills, are provided by someone POSSESSING those skills, and would typically need to be PURCHASED if not donated (e.g. pro bono legal or medical services) — ordinary volunteer labor (e.g. general volunteer fundraising help) is generally NOT recognised, even though it has real economic value. GIFTS-IN-KIND (donated goods, materials, or use of facilities) are recognised at FAIR VALUE when received, with enhanced disclosure requirements (ASU 2020-07) requiring separate presentation from cash contributions and disclosure of the valuation techniques used.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Functional Expense Reporting — the By-Nature-and-Function Matrix</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A genuinely distinctive, mandatory two-dimensional expense presentation</div><div class="principle">Not-for-profits must present expenses analysed by BOTH their NATURE (e.g. salaries, occupancy, supplies) AND their FUNCTION (e.g. program services, management and general, fundraising) — typically as a MATRIX/GRID, either on the face of the financial statements or in the notes. This dual-dimension expense disclosure requirement is genuinely DISTINCTIVE to not-for-profit reporting, reflecting donors' and grantors' particular interest in understanding how much of an organisation's spending goes toward its PROGRAM MISSION versus overhead/fundraising.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Endowments — Donor-Restricted vs Board-Designated Quasi-Endowments</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">A meaningful distinction in restriction source</div><div class="principle">A TRUE DONOR-RESTRICTED endowment is established by a DONOR's explicit stipulation that the principal be maintained in perpetuity (or for a specified term) — classified as NET ASSETS WITH donor restrictions. A "QUASI-ENDOWMENT" (or BOARD-DESIGNATED endowment) is created when the organisation's OWN GOVERNING BOARD elects to set aside otherwise UNRESTRICTED funds to function like an endowment — since this restriction is SELF-IMPOSED (not donor-imposed), quasi-endowment funds remain classified as NET ASSETS WITHOUT donor restrictions, and the board retains the ability to REDIRECT them if circumstances require.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Conditional vs Unconditional Promise to Give</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Applying the barrier test</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A matching-gift pledge</div><div class="wk-body">A donor pledges $500,000 to a nonprofit, CONDITIONED on the nonprofit raising an EQUAL $500,000 in matching gifts from OTHER donors within the next 12 months — with the pledge explicitly RETURNABLE to the donor if the match isn't achieved.</div><div class="wk-answer">The MATCHING requirement is a genuine BARRIER (a specific, measurable condition with a right of RETURN if not met) — this is a <strong>CONDITIONAL</strong> promise to give. NO contribution revenue is recognised UNTIL the nonprofit actually RAISES the matching $500,000 — at which point the condition is substantially met, and the FULL $500,000 pledge is recognised as contribution revenue at that time.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example — Functional Expense Allocation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">The by-nature-and-function matrix in practice</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Allocating a shared executive director's salary</div><div class="wk-body">A nonprofit's executive director spends an estimated 60% of their time on program service delivery, 30% on management and general activities, and 10% on fundraising, with a total annual salary of $150,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">The $150,000 salary (a NATURE category) is ALLOCATED across FUNCTION categories: <strong>$90,000 to program services</strong> (60%), <strong>$45,000 to management and general</strong> (30%), and <strong>$15,000 to fundraising</strong> (10%) — appearing in the functional expense matrix disaggregated both by its NATURE (salary) and its FUNCTIONAL allocation.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 958 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Net assets with and without donor restrictions, and the nature/timing of significant restrictions</span><span>Expenses by both nature and function (the expense matrix)</span><span>Liquidity and availability of financial assets to meet near-term cash needs</span><span>Donated services and gifts-in-kind recognised, including valuation techniques used</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
    <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Distinguishing a genuine barrier-based condition from a mere donor-imposed restriction</span><span>Whether a resource inflow is a contribution (ASC 958) or an exchange transaction (ASC 606)</span><span>Whether donated services meet the specialised-skills recognition criteria</span></div></div>
    <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Fair value of gifts-in-kind and donated services</span><span>Functional expense allocation percentages across shared costs</span></div></div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>No Dedicated IFRS Not-for-Profit Standard — Discussion</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A genuine, substantial gap in the international standards landscape</div><div class="principle">IFRS has NO dedicated, comprehensive standard addressing not-for-profit contribution accounting comparable to ASC 958 — this is a GENUINE, substantial gap, not merely a difference in specific mechanics. IAS 20 addresses GOVERNMENT grants specifically, but general DONOR contribution recognition, net asset classification, and functional expense reporting of the kind ASC 958 provides simply has NO direct IFRS counterpart. Many not-for-profits reporting internationally instead rely on a PATCHWORK of national GAAP requirements, or on International Public Sector Accounting Standards (IPSAS) — a ENTIRELY SEPARATE standard-setting framework from IFRS, primarily developed for public-sector/governmental entities rather than private charitable organisations. This makes ASC 958 one of the more genuinely DISTINCTIVE, comprehensively developed Topics in the entire US GAAP Codification relative to the international standards landscape.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 958 event lands</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Unconditional promise to give</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Recognised as revenue and a receivable when the promise is made</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Conditional promise to give</span><span>None until met</span><span>Recognised only when the barrier condition is substantially met</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Functional expenses</span><span><span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Presented by both nature and function</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 606</span><span>Governs exchange transactions that must be distinguished from genuine nonreciprocal contributions</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 954</span><span>Health Care Entities — many health care providers are also not-for-profit, applying both Topics together</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 820</span><span>Supplies the fair value measurement framework for gifts-in-kind and donated services</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Barrier</div><div class="glossary-def">A specific, measurable condition (such as a matching requirement) that must be overcome before a conditional promise to give is recognised.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Quasi-endowment (board-designated endowment)</div><div class="glossary-def">Funds an organisation's own board designates to function like an endowment, remaining classified as net assets without donor restrictions since the restriction is self-imposed.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Functional expense classification</div><div class="glossary-def">The required presentation of expenses by both their nature and their function (program, management and general, fundraising).</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.asc958 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
